Tars_Tarkas wrote:
> Martin Read wrote:
> 
>> Stanislav Traykov <news@tortoises.org> wrote:
>>
>>> Few suggestions from me too:
>>>
>>> * Abusing wisdom is nonsense in most (all?) scenarios. The player
>>> could have calculated their actions (e.g. seeking to return to
>>> native form.)
>>
>> I *think* there are already actions one might perform in a deliberately
>> calculated fashion that abuse wisdom.
> 
> I think there's hardly any action in netack one might not in some way 
> deliberately perform, abusing wisdom or not. You might even want to 
> abuse wisdom, for who knows what doom might befall you in the dungeons.

Very few actions you might want to perform deliberately under any 
circumstances abuse wisdom AFAICS. And most of those that do, usually 
punish you for something unwise you have done in the past.

It seems to me that if someone became a silver-hating monster and wanted 
to return to their native form, the *wisest* thing to do would be to try 
to pick up a silver object a few times. The game should probably even 
excercise wisdom in this case but as it cannot tell the case, it should 
leave it alone instead of trying to guess from insufficient information.

As an alternative, the wisdom abuse could be conditionalized so that 
only players wearing an amulet of unchanging would be penalized--in any 
other case we cannot tell the player's intent.

